      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-240531",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/c5/c5f3357c38b063be3ad95107be968cb26c03265f7682b132f2fb211a425cdffd.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $P$ is a point equisitant from the points $A$ and $B$, and $l$ is the perpendicular line to $AB^(\u2015)$ through the point $P$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *$l$ is the perpendicular bisector of the segment $AB^(\u2015)$*\n\nThe table below gives the proof of this statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $AP = BP$ | Given |\n| 2 | $AP^(\u2015)\u2245BP^(\u2015)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| 3 | $PM^(\u2015)\u2245PM^(\u2015)$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 4 | $l\u22a5AB^(\u2015)$ | Given |\n| 5 | $m\u2220AMP = 90^{\u2218}$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 6 | $m\u2220BMP = 90^{\u2218}$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 7 | {{field-2}} | HL Criterion |\n| 8 | $AM^(\u2015)\u2245BM^(\u2015)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| 9 | $l$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB^(\u2015)$ | Definition of perpendicular bisector |"
